 ## What an AI client sees, and who decides
 
-The tools return what the web UI shows a reader: instance names, settings, check results, sizes, and statement text as the query_text policy keeps it. Whether that may go to a model hosted elsewhere is the site's decision, made in the client: pgvigil sends nothing anywhere itself (not proven: see Nothing outbound above). A site that may not send statement text to a hosted model sets `query_text: normalized` or `none` for the instances concerned, or uses a model that runs on its own machines.
+The tools return what the web UI shows a reader: instance names, settings, check results, sizes, and statement text as the query_text policy keeps it - with `-config`; started without it, the door reads every instance as `none` and returns no statement text, plan or deadlock graph. Whether that may go to a model hosted elsewhere is the site's decision, made in the client: pgvigil sends nothing anywhere itself (not proven: see Nothing outbound above). A site that may not send statement text to a hosted model sets `query_text: normalized` or `none` for the instances concerned, or uses a model that runs on its own machines.
 
 ## The proof
 
-`scripts/proof-mcp.sh` seeds a demo repository, makes a reader login, and drives one `pgvigil mcp` process with a scripted client: the owner's login refused, `initialize`, `tools/list`, every tool once, a write refused three ways (a tool that would terminate a backend, a write method, and the reader login's own INSERT), the password in no output, no listening socket, and a clean exit when the input closes.
+`scripts/proof-mcp.sh` seeds a demo repository, makes a reader login, and drives a `pgvigil mcp` process with a scripted client: the owner's login refused, `initialize`, `tools/list`, every tool once, a write refused three ways (a tool that would terminate a backend, a write method, and the reader login's own INSERT), the password in no output, no listening socket, and a clean exit when the input closes; then, without `-config` against a second process given a configuration file, no statement text, head statement or plan text returned without it and each of them with it.
diff --git c/docs/security.html i/docs/security.html
index ed70ca82..20fffa00 100644
--- c/docs/security.html
+++ i/docs/security.html
@@ -50,6 +50,7 @@
   <h2>The data it keeps</h2>
   <ul class="list">
     <li>Passwords can stay out of the config file (an inline one is refused for SQL Server and for the actions and EXPLAIN logins, not for a PostgreSQL server's own login). You choose whether statement text is kept as is, with every literal replaced, or not at all. Whichever you choose, on SQL Server and on PostgreSQL, the password-shaped parts of the statements, plans and Extended Events reports it keeps are masked before they are stored: a PASSWORD, SECRET, KEY_SOURCE, IDENTITY_VALUE or IDENTIFIED BY value; a password parameter by name, and every value of a call of sp_addlogin, sp_password, sp_addlinkedsrvlogin or the application-role procedures by position; a connection string's or a URL's password; the -P of a sqlcmd, bcp, osql or isql command line; a key, passphrase or password given to pgcrypto or to SQL Server's passphrase, certificate and asymmetric-key functions; a private key block; a plan's value of a password parameter. A password kept as an ordinary value in a table's data is a literal like any other: only the setting that replaces every literal, or keeps no text, takes it out.</li>
+    <li>The statement-text setting applies when the text is read, not only when it is collected: a page, an export, a report mail, an alert or the MCP door started with the configuration file (<code>pgvigil mcp -config</code>) shows the text an instance's setting allows now, so an instance moved to keep no text stops showing the text kept before, and one moved to replace every literal shows the old text with its literals replaced. Without <code>-config</code> the MCP door knows no setting and reads every instance as keeping no text: it returns no statement text, plan or deadlock graph (its log says so and names <code>-config</code>). Two are the admin's own: a custom report on the store runs its own SQL as the reports login and reads the stored text as it was kept (the Custom reports page says so while any instance keeps less than the full text), and a report mail rendered before a change is sent as it was rendered.</li>
     <li>Its own database gives each part its own role: the collector adds data only through its import functions, the web page reads (and records each action it runs), the alert sender holds a login of its own, and the sign-in check runs through one function nobody else can call.</li>
     <li>The web page has its own sign-in (argon2id password hashes, strict cookies, rate limits) and refuses to listen beyond the local machine without HTTPS.</li>
   </ul>
